= Apple (Malus domestica)

_Scope: This entry treats the domesticated apple, Malus domestica Borkh. (Rosaceae), and its fruit. Apple is the sister pome of the pear (see `pear.typ`) — both Rosaceae with a fleshy hypanthium enclosing the core — and the two are treated in parallel. The classical status is markedly uneven across systems, and the entry is honest about it: apple is deeply classical in Greco-Roman medicine (mālum) and in Tibb (تفاح tuffāḥ, the cardiac cordials), but the modern large-fruited apple is a late (19th–20th c.) Western introduction to China — the classical Chinese pome-drugs are the native crabapples 柰 (nài) and 林檎 (línqín) — and it is largely post-classical in Ayurveda (the Persian-derived seb). Those two sections are reasoned extension, flagged as such._

*Relation to sister fruit:* apple ← Rosaceae pome, sister to pear (`pear.typ`). Both are cooling-sweet, pectin-rich pomes; the traditions grade them closely, with pear read as the more cooling-moistening (the Lung fruit) and apple as the more cardiac-and-balancing (the heart fruit).

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Malus domestica_ Borkh. (family Rosaceae, subfamily Amygdaloideae, tribe Maleae — the pome group); common synonyms _M. pumila_ Mill., _M. communis_ Desf., and the Linnaean _Pyrus malus_ L. Latin _mālum_ (apple) is a loan from Greek *μῆλον (mêlon)*, Doric *μᾶλον (mâlon)* — and *both the Greek and the Latin word meant "apple" and, generically, "tree-fruit / round fruit,"* which set off a great semantic radiation: quince = _mēlon kydōnion_ ("Cydonian apple"), peach = _mālum persicum_ ("Persian apple," whence _persica_ → "peach"), apricot = _mālum armeniacum_, citron/lemon = _mēlon mēdikon_ ("Median apple"), pomegranate = _mālum granatum_ ("seeded apple"), and much later the tomato = Italian _pomodoro_ ("golden apple"). The near-homophony in Latin of _mālum_ (apple, long _ā_) and _malum_ (evil, short _a_) is the pun behind the Western identification of the *unnamed* fruit of Eden (Genesis 3 names no species) as an apple.#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _malum¹_ "apple" and _malum²_ "evil"); Liddell & Scott (s.v. μῆλον, the fruit — distinct from the homograph μῆλον "sheep"); Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_ (μῆλον flagged as possibly pre-Greek). The generic "tree-fruit" sense driving the quince/peach/citron/pomegranate compounds is standard. The _mālum_/_malum_ apple/evil pun and the Edenic-apple identification (the fruit is unnamed in Genesis) are well documented in reception history; the Vulgate's _malum_ at Song of Songs and the tree-of-knowledge iconography fixed the apple — flagged as reception, not botany.]

*English:* _Apple_ — from Old English *æppel*, from Proto-Germanic \*_aplaz_ (cf. German _Apfel_, Dutch _appel_), from a Proto-Indo-European fruit-word \*_h₂ébōl_ / \*_h₂ébel_ (cf. Welsh _afal_, Old Irish _ubull_, Lithuanian _obuolys_). Like _mēlon_, it was originally *generic for fruit* — surviving in "apple of the eye," "earth-apple" (French _pomme de terre_ = potato), and "pine-apple" (originally the pine cone).#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "apple, n."): OE _æppel_ < Proto-Germanic \*_aplaz_ < a north-European PIE fruit word; originally generic "fruit," narrowed to _Malus_. The "apple of the eye," _pomme de terre_, and "pineapple" (< pine cone) preserve the generic sense.]

*Chinese:* the modern cultivated apple (_M. domestica_) is *蘋果 / 苹果 (píngguǒ)* — but it is a *late Western introduction* (西洋蘋果, "Western apple," widely planted only from the late Qing / 19th–20th c.) and is *not* a classical _Bencao_ drug. The name 蘋果 derives from the Buddhist transliteration *頻婆(果) (pínpó(guǒ))*, ultimately a Sanskrit loan. The *classical* Chinese pome-drugs are the *native crabapples*: *柰 (nài)* and *林檎 (línqín, "forest fowl," so named because the sweet fruit drew birds) = _Malus asiatica_ / _M. prunifolia_*, the Chinese/Asiatic crabapple, also *花紅 (huāhóng)* and *沙果 (shāguǒ)* — these have _Bencao_ entries; the modern dessert apple does not.#footnote[蘋果/苹果 (_M. domestica_) as a late Western introduction (西洋蘋果), and its name from Buddhist 頻婆果 (_pínpó_, a Sanskrit transliteration): standard Chinese etymological and horticultural literature. The classical native pome-drugs 柰 (nài) and 林檎 (línqín, _M. asiatica_/_prunifolia_): Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 柰 / 林檎 entries. The modern apple is post-classical in China — flagged; its 中醫 placement below is by extension from the crabapple entries.]

*Sanskrit:* *No core-classical name.* The apple is *post-classical* in India — cultivated anciently only in the Himalayan/Kashmiri fringe, and absent from the Caraka/Suśruta core fruit-lists. The usual modern name *सेब / सेव (seb / seva)* is a *Persian loan* (from سیب _sīb_); Kashmiri and Himalayan usage is old but regional. Treated as reasoned-only below.#footnote[The apple is not among the classical _phala-varga_ fruits of the core saṃhitās; _seb_/_seva_ is Persian-derived (سیب _sīb_). Himalayan/Kashmiri cultivation is old but regional; the dravyaguna reading here is reasoned extension — flagged. Monier-Williams has no core-classical apple lemma of the dessert-apple sense.]

*Arabic / Persian / Urdu (Tibb):* *تفّاح (tuffāḥ)* — apple, a *major and fully classical* Tibb fruit; the single apple is *tuffāḥa*. Persian: *سیب (sīb)*. Urdu/Hakeemi: *سیب (seb)*, *تفاح (tuffāḥ)*. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.#footnote[_Tuffāḥ_ (apple) as a canonical Tibb fruit: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II (_tuffāḥ_); Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_. Persian _sīb_, Urdu _seb_/_tuffāḥ_.]

*Greek:* *μῆλον (mêlon)* — apple (and generic tree-fruit); modern Greek *μήλο (mílo)*.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_The modern apple (蘋果) postdates the Bencao tradition; it has no early entry under that name. The reading below is reasoned extension on classical axes, anchored on the genuinely classical native crabapples 林檎 (línqín) and 柰 (nài) and on modern Chinese food-dietetics — flagged, not canonical Bencao for the dessert apple._

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Cool (涼) to neutral (平); sweet-sour.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘) and sour (酸).

*Channels (by extension):* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺).

*Actions (by extension, with the crabapple classical basis):*
- *生津潤肺 (shēng jīn rùn fèi)* — generates fluids and moistens the Lung (thirst, dry throat, dry cough) — the pome-fruit register shared with pear, though weaker than pear.
- *健脾開胃 (jiàn pí kāi wèi)* — strengthens the Spleen and opens the appetite; *除煩 (chú fán)* — clears vexation/restlessness (heat-vexation, thirst).
- *澀腸止瀉 (sè cháng zhǐ xiè)* — astringes the intestines and stops diarrhea — the classical 林檎 astringent action, and the modern correlate of *pectin*; note the apple's *pectin duality* — raw/ripe apple relieves constipation (moistening), while grated/cooked apple and the pectin check diarrhea.
- *解酒 (jiě jiǔ)* — resolves the effects of alcohol.

*Cautions (by extension):* the cool-moist nature can burden Spleen-Stomach cold-vacuity (loose stools, cold abdomen) if eaten raw and cold in quantity; the classical 林檎 texts also warn that excess causes distension and phlegm.#footnote[Classical 林檎 (línqín): sweet-sour, warm-ish, 止渴 (thirst-quenching) and astringent (澀) for diarrhea, with a caution that excess "generates phlegm and causes distension" — Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_, 林檎 entry. The cool-sweet-sour reading for the modern dessert apple, the pectin-duality (constipation vs diarrhea), and 解酒 are modern Chinese food-dietetic extensions — flagged. Cf. the 潤 glossary entry for 潤肺.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical._ Malus domestica _is post-classical in India (the Persian-derived seb); it is not in the core saṃhitā fruit-lists. The reading below applies dravyaguna principles by extension, in the register of the other reasoned-only entries; the Ayurvedic teaching on raw vs. cooked apple, however, is a living and coherent dietetic application worth stating._

*Reasoned rasa:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent — the pectin/tannin note), a slight Amla (अम्ल, sour) in tart varieties.

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry — the raw apple's roughness), Sīta-sparśa (cooling to the touch).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Śīta (शीत, cooling).

*Reasoned vipāka:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).

*Prabhāva:* None classically (no classical entry). If singled out, the *pectin-mediated bidirectional bowel action* (moistening constipation yet binding diarrhea) is the candidate feature — modern interpretive, flagged.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pitta-śāmaka (sweet-cooling-astringent) and Kapha-neutral; *Vāta-vardhaka when raw* (the rūkṣa-śīta-kaṣāya profile) — hence the classic Ayurvedic dietetic teaching that *raw apple aggravates Vāta and is best eaten cooked/stewed with warming spices (ghee, cinnamon, clove, cardamom)*, which converts it to a Vāta-pacifying, easily-digested food.

*Reasoned karma:* _Grāhī_ (astringent, stool-binding — the pectin), _hṛdya_ (cardiac-benefiting — converging with the Tibb cardiac reading), _rocana / dīpana_ (mild appetizer, esp. cooked), _tṛṣṇā-nigrahaṇa_ (thirst-quenching), _pitta-śāmaka_, _rakta-prasādana_ (blood-cleansing, folk-modern). Cooked apple is _laghu-bṛṃhaṇa_ and easy for the convalescent and the child.#footnote[Reasoned placement; not classical. The *raw-apple-aggravates-Vāta / cooked-apple-pacifies* teaching is a standard living Ayurvedic dietetic principle for cold-rough-light fruits, applied by extension to the post-classical apple — flagged as reasoned. _Grāhī_ per its glossary entry; _hṛdya_ converges with the Tibb _muqawwī al-qalb_ reading.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(no classical viruddha for the post-classical apple — extensions of conditional rules; tags S/N/F/M)_

- *(extended) Conditional:* Vāta excess and cold-rough digestion (raw, cold apple); the _phala + dugdha_ (fruit-with-milk) folk caution applies to raw apple with milk (cooked apple-and-milk, as in apple kheer, is the traditional workaround).
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *FODMAP intolerance / IBS* — apples are high in *fructose and sorbitol*; a common trigger of bloating, gas, and osmotic diarrhea in fructose-malabsorbers and IBS.
  - *Apple-seed amygdalin* — the *seeds* contain the cyanogenic glycoside amygdalin; incidental swallowed seeds are harmless, but deliberately crushing/eating large quantities of seeds can release cyanide — avoid.
  - *Oral allergy syndrome* — apple (Mal d 1) cross-reacts with birch pollen; raw apple can cause oral itching in birch-allergic people (cooking usually abolishes it).
  - *Pesticide residue and dental acid* — apples head the "dirty dozen" residue lists (wash/peel); the sugar-acid is mildly cariogenic.#footnote[FODMAP (fructose/sorbitol) intolerance, apple-seed amygdalin/cyanide, birch-associated oral allergy syndrome (Mal d 1), and pesticide-residue/dental points are modern food-science overlays — flagged (M), not classical; verify against current literature.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Apple (*تفّاح tuffāḥ*) is *fully classical and highly regarded* in Tibb — above all as a *cardiac tonic and exhilarant*: the apple, and even its *scent*, is held to gladden and strengthen the heart and the vital spirit. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.

*Arabic:* *تفّاح (tuffāḥ)*; Persian *سیب (sīb)*; Urdu/Hakeemi *سیب (seb)*, *تفاح (tuffāḥ)*.

*Mizāj:* *variety-graded* — the *sweet* apple is *temperate, tending Hot-and-Moist* in the 1st degree; the *sour* apple is *Cold-and-Dry*; the general/unripe apple leans cool. Avicenna treats the types separately.#footnote[Mizāj (sweet apple temperate/warm-moist; sour apple cold-dry) and the cardiac reading of _tuffāḥ_: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II (_tuffāḥ_); Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_. The degree assignments vary by variety and edition — flagged for verification against a specific edition.]

*Afʿāl (actions):*
- *Muqawwī al-qalb* — cardiac tonic; *mufarriḥ* — exhilarant / gladdener of the heart and spirit (the signature action; the basis of the Tuffāḥī cordials).
- *Muqawwī al-dimāgh* — brain/nerve tonic; *muqawwī al-maʿida* — stomach tonic; the *sour* apple is *qābiḍ* (astringent) and stomach-toning for bilious nausea.
- *Muʿaṭṭir / munʿish* — the *apple scent* (_shamm al-tuffāḥ_) as a reviving aromatic for faintness, palpitation, and low spirits.
- *Mufattiḥ* (mild) and *mudirr* (mild diuretic) in some readings.

*Istemālāt (indications):* palpitation, cardiac weakness, and low spirits/melancholy; nervous debility; bilious stomach and nausea (sour apple); faintness (apple scent); convalescence.

*Mawāneʿ / Iḥtirāzāt (contraindications):* excess raw apple is held to generate wind (_riyāḥ_) and colic and to be slow to digest; cold constitutions should prefer the sweet, ripe, or cooked apple.

*Muṣliḥ (correctives):* honey, sugar, and warm aromatics (cinnamon, clove) — exactly the spicing of apple preserves and cordials. *Badal (substitute):* quince (_safarjal_) for the astringent-cardiac-stomachic action; pear (_kummathrā_) for the cooling register.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* apple is a *Greco-Arabic (Avicennan) drug*, not a Qurʾānic or firmly-hadith substance — flagged honestly, though it pervades later Muslim dietetics and the Mughal-Unani cordial tradition.

*Classical Tibb preparations:* *Sharbat-e Sīb* (apple syrup/cordial), *Murabbā-e Sīb* (apple preserve), and the *Tuffāḥī* class of cardiac cordials (e.g. the compound _Sharbat-e Tuffāḥ_ / _Jawārish_-class and _Khamīrah_-class cardiacs in which apple is the gladdening base); *shamm al-tuffāḥ*, apple-smelling for the heart.#footnote[_Sharbat-e Sīb_, _Murabbā-e Sīb_, the Tuffāḥī cardiac cordials, and _shamm al-tuffāḥ_ (apple-scent for the heart): Unani formulary tradition (AYUSH, _National Formulary of Unani Medicine_); Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II. The cardiac/exhilarant register is the apple's signature Tibb use. See the _mufariḥ_ and _muqawwī al-qalb_ glossary entries.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formula uses the modern apple. Classical native-crabapple use (林檎, 柰) is dietetic and minor — fresh fruit for thirst, dried/astringent for diarrhea; 林檎 also candied and preserved. Modern 蘋果 use is food-therapy (食療): apple-and-rock-sugar stews, apple in tonic soups, and grated apple for infant diarrhea (the pectin) — post-classical.#footnote[Classical 林檎/柰 dietetic and preserved use: _Bencao Gangmu_, 林檎/柰 entries. Modern apple food-therapy (grated apple for diarrhea, apple-rock-sugar stew) is post-classical Chinese dietetics — flagged.]

=== Ayurveda

No classical formula. The living dietetic preparation is *stewed/spiced apple* (apple cooked soft with ghee and warming spices — cinnamon, clove, cardamom), the Vāta-pacifying convalescent and infant form; *apple kheer* (cooked apple-and-milk) circumvents the raw-fruit-with-milk caution. Post-classical, flagged.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman:* *Dioscorides* (_De Materia Medica_ I, on _mêla_) and *Galen* grade apples by sweetness and ripeness — sweet ripe apples nourishing and cordial, sour/unripe apples astringent and cooling, best cooked; *Pliny* (_Naturalis Historia_ XV, XXIII) catalogues many apple varieties and their uses. Apple carried cardiac and cheering associations from antiquity, and a dense *mythic* register: the golden apples of the Hesperides, the Apple of Discord and the Judgment of Paris, and Atalanta's race.
- *Medieval / early-modern:* the *Regimen Sanitatis Salernitanum* and the humoral tradition treat apples (often advising them cooked/roasted for the stomach); the *baked apple* is a classic convalescent food; *verjuice* (_verjus_) from sour crabapples and grapes was a kitchen-and-pharmacy acidulant; the pomander (_pomum ambrae_) name preserves _pomum_, "apple/fruit."
- *Cider and cider vinegar:* fermented *cider* (and _applejack_) and *apple cider vinegar* are the great apple preparations of northern Europe and North America; ACV carries a large modern folk-medical claim-set (glycemic, weight, "detox") that is *mostly overstated* — modest glycemic evidence at best, flagged.
- *"An apple a day":* the 19th-c. English proverb crystallizes the apple's health reputation; modern epidemiology finds apple/flavonoid intake modestly associated with cardiovascular and metabolic benefit — real but modest, and not the panacea of folklore.#footnote[Greco-Roman: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ I (Beck translation); Galen, _On the Properties of Foodstuffs_; Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XV, XXIII (Loeb). Salernitan regimen and baked-apple/verjuice: standard food- and pharmacy-history (Dalby, _Food in the Ancient World_). Cider vinegar health claims are largely overstated (modest glycemic data) — flagged. "An apple a day" (Welsh/English 19th-c. proverb) and the modest modern flavonoid epidemiology — flagged as modest.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

Covered above: *Sharbat-e Sīb*, *Murabbā-e Sīb*, the Tuffāḥī cardiac cordials, and *shamm al-tuffāḥ* (apple-scent for the heart).

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

The apple is the great *cool-temperate, winter-chill-requiring* pome. Its wild progenitor is *Malus sieversii* of the *Tian Shan* (Kazakhstan — Almaty, "father of apples"), whence it spread west along the Silk Road, hybridizing with European crabapple. Unlike Mediterranean or tropical fruit, the apple *needs winter cold* (chill-hours to break bud dormancy) and fruits in autumn — a genuinely *cold-winter continental-temperate* crop. See `climates.typ`.

=== Cool-temperate continental (the home)

Central Asian, European, North American, and East-Asian-highland temperate zones. Constitutionally these carry cold-dry winters (Vāta; external 寒/燥) and heat-and-damp summers. *Traditional preparation and cares.* The autumn apple is stored through winter (the classic cellared keeping-fruit) and is eaten *raw in season* (cooling, thirst-quenching) but *cooked in the cold months* — baked, stewed, in pies, sauces, and compotes with warming spices — exactly the _saṃskāra_ shift the constitutional logic wants (raw-cool → cooked-warm for the cold season and for weak/cold digestion). Fermented *cider* is the northern cool-damp transposition (raw-cool juice → warming ferment), in the same logic as the beer/kvass ferments treated in `climates.typ`.

=== Modern transposition (_kāla-viruddha_ / _deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

The apple is now pan-climatic and eaten raw year-round, including cold-and-raw in winter and in cold/Vāta constitutions where the classical (Ayurvedic and Tibb) counsel is the *cooked, spiced* form — a _kāla-viruddha_ (wrong-season/raw-in-cold) drift. The other modern transposition is the *cider-vinegar-as-cure-all* register, which detaches the fruit from food and inflates its claims. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Apple is a *whole food*; the relevant figures are dietary, not toxicological.

*Composition (per 100 g, raw with skin):*
- Energy ~52 kcal; water ~86 g.
- Carbohydrate ~14 g, of which *sugars ~10 g* (fructose-dominant, with glucose and sucrose) and *fiber ~2.4 g*.
- *Pectin* — the characteristic soluble fiber (much of it in the flesh and just under the skin); the basis of the bowel-regulating and modest cholesterol-lowering effects.
- Vitamin C (modest, mostly in the skin), potassium; low protein and fat.
- *Polyphenols* — *quercetin* glycosides (concentrated in the *peel*), catechins, chlorogenic acid, phloridzin (an apple-specific dihydrochalcone); the antioxidant fraction, largely lost when peeled.#footnote[Composition: USDA FoodData Central (apple, raw, with skin). Pectin (soluble fiber), peel-concentrated quercetin and the apple-specific phloridzin are characteristic; verify current figures.]

*Pharmacology / physiology of note:*
- *Bowel-regulating (bidirectional)* — pectin both softens/bulk-forms (relieving constipation) and, as a gel, binds and slows (checking mild diarrhea) — the modern correlate of the classical constipation/diarrhea duality and the grated-apple infant-diarrhea folk use.
- *Glycemic / lipid* — soluble fiber and polyphenols give a modest glycemic-flattening and small LDL-cholesterol-lowering effect with regular whole-apple (not juice) intake.
- *Cardiometabolic epidemiology* — apple/flavonoid intake is modestly associated with lower cardiovascular risk (the "apple a day" signal) — real but modest; whole fruit outperforms juice.
- *Dental/FODMAP caveats* — sugar-acid is mildly cariogenic; fructose/sorbitol trigger IBS/FODMAP symptoms in susceptible people.

*Safe daily intake / practical figure:* no toxicological ceiling for the fruit.
- *Practical portion:* 1–2 medium apples/day is an unremarkable, healthful intake; whole fruit is preferable to juice (juice loses fiber and concentrates sugar).
- *Cautions / risk groups:* IBS/fructose-malabsorption (bloating); birch-pollen oral allergy (raw apple); *do not eat quantities of crushed seeds* (amygdalin/cyanide); wash/peel for residue if not organic.
- *Reference/dietary information, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Bowel, glycemic/lipid, and cardiometabolic data: current nutrition literature — modest effects, whole fruit > juice; flagged. FODMAP, oral-allergy, and apple-seed-cyanide cautions per food-science/toxicology literature. Dietary reference points, not medical advice; verify current figures.]

